    "result_text": "The requirement is already fully satisfied by the existing code, and I've now verified every rejection path directly.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change \u2014 **all 400/409/429 rejection responses return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`** \u2014 is implemented in `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`.\n\nEvery rejection flows through `@ExceptionHandler` methods there, each of which builds a Spring `ProblemDetail` via the shared `detailBuild(...)` helper (`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java:71`) and returns it as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`. That helper populates all four required members:\n- `type` \u2014 `problemDetail.setType(...)`\n- `title` \u2014 `problemDetail.setTitle(...)`\n- `status` \u2014 set by `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)`\n- `detail` \u2014 `problemDetail.setDetail(...)`\n\nReturning a `ProblemDetail` body makes Spring MVC negotiate the `application/problem+json` content type automatically, and the HTTP status codes are unchanged (400 for validation/missing/invalid fields, 409 for the duplicate/city-capacity conflicts, 429 for the daily limit).\n\n### Verification\n- Full acceptance suite: **71 tests, 0 failures** (`Cp01Tests`\u2013`Cp59Tests`), including `Cp59Tests` which asserts a 400 rejection is `application/problem+json` with `$.status == 400`.\n- Because the 409 and 429 paths had no dedicated problem+json test, I added a temporary test exercising both, confirmed each returns `application/problem+json` with `type`/`title`/`status`/`detail` present and `status` matching the code, then removed the temporary test.\n\nThe test suite is green; no source changes were needed.",
